1. Conditionals

Conditionals 条件语句 allow a program to make decisions and execute different blocks of code depending on whether a condition is true or false.

1.1 if Statement

An if statement executes its code block only when the condition is true.

Syntax:

if (condition)
{
    // Code to execute if the condition is true
}

Example:

int x = 2;
int y = 3;

if (x < y)
{
    printf("x is less than y\n");
}

Since 2 < 3 is true, the program prints:

x is less than y

Indentation: Code inside the curly braces { } is usually indented by 4 spaces to make it easier to read.

1.2 if-else Statement

An if-else statement provides two possible paths:

If the condition is true, execute the if block.

Otherwise, execute the else block.

Example:

if (x < y)
{
    printf("x is less than y\n");
}
else
{
    printf("x is not less than y\n");
}

1.3 if-else if-else Statement

When comparing two numbers, there are three possible relationships:

x < y

x > y

x == y

Example:

if (x < y)
{
    printf("x is less than y\n");
}
else if (x > y)
{
    printf("x is greater than y\n");
}
else
{
    printf("x is equal to y\n");
}

The final else handles the remaining possibility: x == y.

This is more concise than writing another else if (x == y) because if the first two conditions are false, the numbers must be equal.

