3.1 bool

Stores one of two values: true or false.

Useful for conditions and decision-making.

3.2 char

Represents a single character, such as 'A', 'b', or '7'.

Different from a string, which can contain multiple characters.

3.3 float

Represents floating-point numbers (numbers with decimal places).

Typically uses 32 bits of memory.

Can represent a wide range of values, but has limited precision.

3.4 double

Also represents floating-point numbers.

Typically uses 64 bits of memory.

Provides greater precision than float.

3.5 int

Represents integers, including positive numbers, zero, and negative numbers.

Typically uses 32 bits of memory.

3.6 long

Represents larger integer values than int on many systems.

Its exact size depends on the computer system and compiler.

3.7 string

Represents text consisting of zero or more characters.

For example: "Hello".

Note: string is a CS50-specific type. In standard C, strings are represented using character arrays or character pointers.

4. Format Specifiers

Format specifiers are used inside printf() to indicate what type of value should be printed.

Format Specifier

Meaning

%c

Print a single character (char)

%f

Print a floating-point value

%i

Print an integer (int)

%li

Print a long integer (long)

%s

Print a string

Examples

Print a character:

char letter = 'A';
printf("%c\n", letter);

Print a floating-point number:

float price = 9.99;
printf("%f\n", price);

Print an integer:

int age = 16;
printf("%i\n", age);

Print a long integer:

long population = 8000000000;
printf("%li\n", population);

Print a string:

string name = "April";
printf("%s\n", name);

Key Takeaways

Conditionals allow programs to make decisions.

if, else if, and else control which code gets executed.

= assigns a value, while == compares two values.

! represents logical NOT.

Different data types store different kinds of values.

Format specifiers such as %c, %f, %i, %li, and %s tell printf() what type of value to print.
